// VgScript Example scenes

[S1] The Room

// A1 is the scene handler: a Blueprint action in the showcase level. It stays alive during the scene,
// sets the room up and opens and closes the laptop when triggered. &(...) means it starts C1 alongside it.
[A1] RoomHandler &(C1)

// A condition group runs the first entry whose condition holds; each entry names its target directly.
// The last entry has no condition: it is the "else". CF{...} checks a fact.
// Facts outlive the scene: scene 2 stores "second-act" before it starts this scene again.
[C1]
[1] if(CF{second-act} && CF{laptop-open}) A2
[2] A3

// Trigger sends a value to a running action, in this case the scene handler switches the laptop screen on.
[A2] Trigger{A1, screen-on} ->(A3)

// Move is the showcase's own action, a Blueprint registered in DA_ShowcaseRegistry:
// Move{who, where} walks a character to a spot in the level and finishes when it arrives.
// &(...) starts Gee's walk at the same time, and ->(T1) would be waiting for Vee alone, here.
[A3] Move{Vee, Left} &(Move{Gee, Right}) ->(T1)

// [P] names who speaks the talks and decisions below it; "->" names who they speak to.
[P] Vee -> Gee
[T1]
[1] Hi, Gee. ->(T2)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T2]
[1] Hi, Vee. ->(T3)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T3]
[1] Are we early? ->(T4)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T4]
[1] For what? ->(T5)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T5]
[1] I don't know. It's only us. ->(T6)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T6]
[1] And a laptop. ->(T7)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T7]
[1] Yes, and that.
[2] So... should we wait? ->(T8)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T8]
[1] For what? ->(T9)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T9]
[1] For whatever we're early for. ->(D1)

// The player chooses what Gee says. A quoted option reuses the first line of the talk it leads to,
// so the choice and the spoken line share one translation. An option's if(...) hides it until the condition is met.
// {hold} asks the player to hold the option to confirm it.

[P] Gee -> Vee
[D1]
[1] 'We could come back later.' ->(T10)
[2] 'I think we've been here before.' ->(T12)
[3] 'What about the laptop?' ->(T16)
[4] if(CF{second-act}) 'You're going to say the laptop is what we're early for.' ->(T18)

[T10]
[1] We could come back later. ->(T11)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T11]
[1] And miss it? ->(T22)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T12]
[1] I think we've been here before. ->(C2)

[C2]
[1] if(CF{second-act}) T15
[2] T13

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T13]
[1] Why would you say that? ->(T14)

// A speaker without "->" talks to nobody in particular.
[P] Gee
[T14]
[1] I don't know why I said that. ->(T22)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T15]
[1] Were we? ->(T22)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T16]
[1] What about the laptop? ->(T17)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T17]
[1] Maybe that's what we're early for. ->(C3)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T18]
[1] You're going to say the laptop is what we're early for. ->(T19)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T19]
[1] Maybe the lap...
[2] How did you know I'd say that? ->(T20)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T20]
[1] You always do. ->(T21)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T21]
[1] Always? We just got here. ->(C3)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T22]
[1] Maybe the laptop is what we're early for. ->(C3)

[C3]
[1] if(CF{second-act} && CF{laptop-open}) T23
[2] D2

[T23]
[1] And it's on. Maybe we're late. ->(D2)

// {!repeat} hides an option once picked, in this pass or an earlier one.
[P] Gee -> Vee
[D2]
[1] 'Let's have a look.' ->(T24)
[2] {!repeat} 'Why did we come in here?' ->(T25)
[3] if(CF{second-act}) {!repeat} 'It's going to ask for a password.' ->(T34)

[T24]
[1] Let's have a look. ->(A4)

// A comma starts both targets in order, without waiting: the fact is stored and Vee answers.
[T25]
[1] Why did we come in here? ->(AddFact{asked-why}, T26)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T26]
[1] You came in, so I came in. ->(T27)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T27]
[1] I came in because you did. ->(T28)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T28]
[1] ...Then who came in first? ->(D3)

// We can execute an action written inline, with no [A] entry of its own.
// Timer{L, T31} keeps six seconds of silence, then starts T31, it's an abbreviation of Timer{L} ->(T31).
// Using "L" is just to be able to change all variables using it at once in editor, without "magic numbers" in the script.
[P] Gee -> Vee
[D3]
[1] 'You did.' ->(T29)
[2] 'Me. By a nose.' ->(T32)
[3] Say nothing. ->(Timer{L, T31})

[T29]
[1] You did. ->(T30)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T30]
[1] I did not. You did. ->(D2)

[T31]
[1] ...So we came in together. ->(D2)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T32]
[1] Me. By a nose. ->(Timer{M, T33})

// Example of a nested flow: wait two seconds, then Vee walks to the laptop. It's "->" inside  "->".
[P] Vee -> Gee
[T33]
[1] Why are you being weird?
[2] Forget it. I'm looking at the laptop. ->(Timer{2} ->(A4))

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T34]
[1] It's going to ask for a password. ->(T35)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T35]
[1] How would you know? You haven't touched it. ->(D2)

// Vee walks to the laptop first. Then, in order and without waiting: the fact is stored, Trigger asks the
// scene handler to open the laptop, and Event waits until the handler raises "screen-ready" before T36 starts.
[A4] Move{Vee, Laptop} ->(AddFact{laptop-open}, Trigger{A1, screen-on}, Event{A1, screen-ready} ->(T36))

// {hold} at the end of a talk line keeps it on screen after it ends, until the next line shows:
// "Three letters." stays up while the player picks a guess.
[P] Vee -> Gee
[T36]
[1] It's locked. It wants a password.
[2] Three letters. {hold} ->(D4)

// A decision can lead back to itself: every wrong guess returns here,
// and {!repeat} drops it once tried, until only the password and "I don't know..." are left.
// The right guess also stores a fact, so the laptop scene can react to it.
[P] Gee -> Vee
[D4]
[1] {!repeat} 'A-B-C?' ->(T37)
[2] {!repeat} 'G-E-E?' ->(T39)
[3] {!repeat} 'V-E-E?' ->(T41)
[4] {!repeat} 'V-L-G?' ->(T43)
[5] 'V-G-S.' ->(T45)
[6] 'I don't know...' ->(T46)

[T37]
[1] A-B-C? ->(T38)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T38]
[1] No. Who would pick that? ->(D4)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T39]
[1] G-E-E? ->(T40)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T40]
[1] No. Not everything is about you, Gee. ->(D4)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T41]
[1] V-E-E? ->(T42)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T42]
[1] No. But it's nice you thought of me. ->(D4)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T43]
[1] V-L-G?
[2] Vee, Laptop, Gee. ->(T44)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T44]
[1] Of course not.
[2] What are the odds the password is exactly what's in this room? ->(D4)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T45]
[1] V-G-S. ->(AddFact{guessed-it}, T47)

[T46]
[1] I don't know... ->(T48)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T47]
[1] V... G... S.
[2] V and G are us. Who's S?
[3] Wait, it... ->(A5)

[T48]
[1] Neither do I.
[2] I'll just press something. ->(A5)

// Two talks at once: Node starts both, and {waitParallel} waits for both to end. A talk only plays over another
// speaker's talk when it carries {parallel}, written on its own line under the talk; without it, it waits its turn.
[A5] Node{waitParallel} &(T49, T50) ->(T51)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T49]
[1] It opened!
{parallel}

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T50]
[1] No way.
{parallel}

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T51]
[1] There's a word at the top.
[2] "VgScript." ->(C4)

[C4]
[1] if(CF{guessed-it}) T52
[2] T53

// RemoveFact clears the guess, so the next act asks again.
[T52]
[1] That's what V-G-S stands for... You guessed it.
[2] How did you know? ->(RemoveFact{guessed-it}, C5)

[T53]
[1] V, G, S. That's what I pressed.
[2] I wasn't even looking. ->(C6)

[C5]
[1] if(CF{second-act}) T54
[2] T55

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T54]
[1] You read it out. Last time. ->(T85)

[T55]
[1] I didn't. It just came out. ->(T56)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T56]
[1] Well, it came out right. ->(C6)

[C6]
[1] if(CF{second-act}) T84
[2] T57

[T57]
[1] Under "VgScript" there's a file. "S1_Room.vgs".
[2] ...It's us. Everything we've said, written down.
[3] "Hi, Gee." "Hi, Vee."
[4] "Are we early?" "For what?"
[5] "I don't know. It's only us." "And a laptop."
[6] A bit further down there's a line of yours. Except there are three:
[7] "We could come back later." "I think we've been here before." "What about the laptop?" ->(C7)

// if(T10) checks a talk, so the group follows what the player had Gee say.
[P] Gee -> Vee
[C7]
[1] if(T10) T58
[2] if(T12) T59
[3] T60

[T58]
[1] I said the first one. ->(T61)

[T59]
[1] I said the second one. ->(T61)

[T60]
[1] I said the last one. ->(T61)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T61]
[1] So why are the other two there? ->(T62)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T62]
[1] In case I said them? ->(T63)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T63]
[1] Mine only have one each.
[2] So you get to pick, and I don't? ->(C8)

[C8]
[1] if(CF{second-act}) T65
[2] T64

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T64]
[1] I didn't pick anything. I just said it. ->(D5)

[T65]
[1] I don't pick either. Someone else does. ->(D5)

// || means "or", && means "and". if!(...) negates the whole condition.
// T32 checks that a talk has played, in this pass or an earlier one.
[D5]
[1] if(CF{asked-why} || T32) 'Then you're the one picking.' ->(T66)
[2] if!(CF{asked-why}) 'Read the rest.' ->(T67)
[3] Close the laptop. ->(Move{Gee, Laptop} ->(RemoveFact{laptop-open}, Trigger{A1, screen-off}, T78))

[T66]
[1] Then you're the one picking. ->(T68)

[T67]
[1] Read the rest. ->(T69)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T68]
[1] Me? I don't even get to pick mine. ->(T69)

[T69]
[1] There are notes between our lines.
[2] "The player chooses what Gee says." ->(C9)

[C9]
[1] if(CF{second-act}) T70
[2] if(T32) T71
[3] T76

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T70]
[1] Told you. ->(T77)

[T71]
[1] So that's why I said "by a nose". ->(T72)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T72]
[1] Somebody picked that? On purpose? ->(T73)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T73]
[1] It's funny. We don't even have noses. ->(T74)

// Two talks at once without a Node: Vee's line starts Gee's talk with &(...), and {waitParallel} holds the line's
// ->(...) until that talk ends. Write it on the line itself; on its own line under the talk, like {parallel}, it does nothing.
[P] Vee -> Gee
[T74]
[1] Ha! {waitParallel} &(T75) ->(T77)
{parallel}

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T75]
[1] Ha ha!
{parallel}

[T76]
[1] The player? ->(T77)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T77]
[1] And right at the top: "VgScript Example scenes."
[2] We're an example, Gee. ->(T79)

[T78]
[1] Too late. I saw the notes: "The player chooses what Gee says."
[2] And the top: "VgScript Example scenes." We're an example, Gee. ->(T79)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T79]
[1] An example of what? ->(T80)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T80]
[1] Of two people talking, I guess. ->(T81)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T81]
[1] Then next time, let's be funnier. ->(T82)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T82]
[1] Don't tell me. The player's the one picking. ->(A6)

[A6] Node{waitParallel} &(T83, Timer{L}) ->(A7)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T83] And they can start us over whenever they like.

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T84]
[1] Same as last time. ->(T85)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T85]
[1] Last time? ->(D6)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[D6]
[1] 'Read it. You'll see.' ->(T86)
[2] {hold} Close the laptop. ->(Move{Gee, Laptop} ->(RemoveFact{laptop-open}, Trigger{A1, screen-off}, T88))

[T86]
[1] Read it. You'll see. ->(T87)

[P] Vee -> Gee
[T87]
[1] "Hi, Gee." "Hi, Vee."... It's us. Everything we've said, in a file.
[2] And under your name there's a list of things you could say. ->(T63)

[T88]
[1] Hey, I was reading that. ->(T89)

[P] Gee -> Vee
[T89]
[1] I know how it ends. We start over. ->(A7)

// Stop the scene handler and jump to the next scene. Write it as ->(S2-A1);
// "vgs resolve-links" swaps it for the destination's stable hash, so renumbering scene 2 never breaks the jump.
[A7] Cancel{A1} ->(F4C4FE7183044E7DA63DD7EA4653B788 /*The End S2-A1*/)